@loom 1.0
!goal Migrate the billing service from the legacy Stripe SDK to the new payments client, and make sure that the migration is fully backwards compatible for all existing webhook consumers, without any downtime for the checkout flow, and without changing the shape of the events that we publish to the internal bus.
!done All existing webhook consumers continue to receive events with the same shape, the checkout flow handles at least 200 requests per second in the load test, and the staging environment has been running on the new client for 48 hours without a single error in the payments dashboard.
!must keep the legacy event schema for at least two release cycles
!never change the public webhook signature verification path
!never rotate the Stripe API keys during the migration window
!never any downtime for the checkout flow, including during the cutover
!must keep the feature flag `billing.new_client` defaulting to off
!never delete the legacy adapter until the two release cycles have passed
#state
  repo = acme/billing at branch release/2026.10
  migration_stage = shadow traffic at 10 percent
  legacy_adapter = still the source of truth for webhooks
  new_client_version = 4.2.1
  feature_flag_default = off
#evidence{id,src,trust,text}
  e1,,0.90,The staging load test reached 180 requests per second before the p99 latency crossed 400 milliseconds
  e2,,0.85,The legacy adapter adds roughly 12 milliseconds of overhead per webhook
  e3,,0.95,The new client emits `payment_intent.succeeded` with a slightly different field ordering but the same fields
  e4,,0.80,"The shadow traffic run produced 3 mismatches out of 10,000 events"
#next
  - Add a schema-equality assertion to the shadow traffic comparator.
  - Raise shadow traffic to 50 percent for 24 hours.
  - Profile the new client under the load test and find the p99 regression.
  - Enable the feature flag in staging only.
  - Document the rollback procedure before touching production.
?q Do any webhook consumers depend on field ordering rather than field names?
?q Is the 200 requests per second target measured at the edge or at the service?
?q Does the new client retry idempotently on 5xx responses?
